<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kalamukha</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kalamukhas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kalamukhas were a Shaiva ascetic sect of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, closely related to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashupata-shaivism">Pashupatas</a>, that flourished especially in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> between the ninth and thirteenth centuries. Their priests ran large temples and monastic colleges, such as those at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balligave">Balligave</a>, teaching grammar, philosophy and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a> with generous support from the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysalas</a>. The sect declined after the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingayatism">Lingayats</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kalamukha.jpg" alt="Kalamukha" width="320" height="309" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vidaikodiselvar S. Danabala · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SaivismFlag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>What is known of the Kalamukhas comes from inscriptions recording grants to their temples and from texts usually written by their opponents. They appear to have been an offshoot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashupata-shaivism">Pashupata</a> sect, about which more is known. Their name comes from kālāmukha, which can mean 'facing the time', 'facing death' or 'black-face'. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a> and similar texts show that they were also known as Laguda, Lakula and Nakula, and link them with other words meaning black-faced, such as Kalanana.</p><p>Their rise to influence coincided with the spread of the agama texts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaivism">Shaivism</a>, and the Tirumandiram names them as one of six schools of thought that emerged from the agamas. Their teaching did not rest on the agamas alone: they also followed the orthodox Śruti and were versed in the Vaisheshika and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyaya">Nyaya</a> schools. The sect had at least two divisions, the Saktiparisad, found over a wide area, and the Simhaparisad, concentrated mostly around the districts of Dharwar and Shimoga.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to Ramendra Nath Nandi, the first known record of the sect is a grant issued by the Rashtrakuta dynasty in 807 CE, though he also refers to a Kalamukha monastery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> in the 8th century. Later records name a Kalamukha temple at Srinivaspur in 870, a temple with a college at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chitradurga">Chitradurga</a> in 947, and a monastery and temple at Nanjangud by 977. Their main centre was Balligavi, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayawada">Vijayawada</a> was among their other temple sites. After a gap of about two centuries in the evidence, they are well documented until the Vijayanagara era.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Vaishnavite teacher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramanuja">Ramanuja</a>, in his Sri Bhasya, described the Kalamukhas as eating from a skull and keeping wine. Such practices were common among the Kapalikas but not typical of the Kalamukhas, and he may have confused the two sects. His account may have been coloured by his own school, by his having been forced by the Kalamukhas and other Shaivite groups to leave his native <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, and perhaps by fear or jealousy of their growing popularity. Nandi describes the Kalamukhas as a sect of social and religious reformers with a strong social basis, unlike the Kapalikas.</p><p>For many years scholars such as R. G. Bhandarkar nonetheless held the Kalamukhas to be a more extreme sect than the Kapalikas, while acknowledging that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramanuja">Ramanuja</a>'s account was confused. David Lorenzen attributes this error to the weight given to Ramanuja's skewed record over the evidence of the inscriptions then collected.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kalamukha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Shaiva sects, including the <b>Kalamukha</b> ascetics who ran large temple colleges at Balligave and elsewhere, enjoyed strong royal patronage; Jainism remained influential, especially among merchants and noblewomen such as Attimabbe, who sponsored copies of Ponna’s works and a Jain temple at Lakkundi; and Vaishnavism grew.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
